Title:
PINKPANTHER – Windows x64 handcrafted token-stealing kernel-mode shellcode (SYSTEM EoP)

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- PINKPANTHER is a Windows x64 kernel-mode, position-independent shellcode payload that elevates privileges by replacing the current process’s primary access token with the SYSTEM process token (token stealing).  
- It targets post-exploitation/EoP scenarios where an attacker already has kernel code execution (e.g., via a kernel exploit or vulnerable driver) and needs a compact privilege escalation payload.  
- The repo focuses on handcrafted assembly shellcode and OS-build-specific structure offsets across many Windows versions (Win7 through Win10 21H2).  
- It discusses practical kernel-internals caveats, notably race conditions when walking the active process list via linked EPROCESS structures without proper locking/object referencing.  
- Suggested safer alternatives include using ZwQuerySystemInformation(SystemProcessInformation) + PsLookupProcessByProcessId and proper object referencing (ObReferenceObject/ObDereferenceObject), at the cost of larger payload size due to symbol resolution needs.  
- Useful for kernel exploit developers, red team operators building EoP chains, and defenders studying common token-stealing primitives.

Technical Focus:
- Windows kernel token stealing (EPROCESS token swap)
- Handcrafted x64 kernel shellcode / position-independent code
- EPROCESS/KTHREAD structure offsets across Windows builds
- ActiveProcessLinks process list walking and synchronization issues
- Kernel APIs: ZwQuerySystemInformation, PsLookupProcessByProcessId, ObReferenceObject
- Payload deployment/testing via kernel shellcode loaders and kernel debugging

Use Cases:
- Integrate as the privilege-escalation payload stage in a Windows kernel exploit
- Replace/standardize token-stealing payloads across multiple Windows versions/builds
- Lab testing of kernel exploitation chains using Hyper-V + kernel debugging
- Defensive research: understand and detect common kernel token manipulation patterns

Keywords:
Windows kernel, x64, kernel-mode shellcode, token stealing, EoP, SYSTEM token, _EPROCESS, _KTHREAD, ActiveProcessLinks, PsLookupProcessByProcessId, ZwQuerySystemInformation, SystemProcessInformation, ObReferenceObject, pushlock, PspActiveProcessLock, race condition, MASM, WDK, ntoskrnl.exe, kernel exploit payload